      <article><strong>11</strong><span>qualified product demos</span></article>
      <article><strong>4</strong><span>serious opportunities</span></article>
      <article><strong>2</strong><span>customers within six months</span></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-engagement">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The engagement</div>
      <h2>A valuable data platform needed a simpler commercial story.</h2>
      <p class="saas-intro">The client provided a cloud based commercial real estate and location intelligence platform. Buyers could examine property information, demographic patterns, market conditions, and location context without assembling the answer manually across disconnected sources. The product was useful. The first challenge was making that usefulness obvious before asking for a demonstration.</p>
      <div class="saas-facts">
        <article><span>Company</span><h3>Data intelligence SaaS platform</h3><p>A confidential software company helping property and location professionals research markets, evaluate opportunities, and support decisions with better information.</p></article>
        <article><span>Target market</span><h3>Commercial real estate and location teams</h3><p>Brokers, developers, site selection professionals, economic development teams, and organizations making location based decisions.</p></article>
        <article><span>Primary outcome</span><h3>Qualified product demonstrations</h3><p>Demos with relevant buyers who understood the platform use case and had a credible reason to examine the software.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-problem">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The commercial problem</div>
      <h2>The platform had plenty of data. Buyers needed to understand the missing decision.</h2>
      <p class="saas-intro">Leading with datasets, dashboards, filters, reports, and software capabilities created too much work for a cold prospect. The campaign needed to connect the platform to a familiar moment: researching a property, comparing a market, supporting a client recommendation, or deciding where an opportunity deserved attention.</p>
      <div class="saas-problem-grid">
        <article><strong>01</strong><h3>Too many features too early</h3><p>The product could do several useful things, but a long feature list made the opening difficult to understand and easy to postpone.</p></article>
        <article><strong>02</strong><h3>Fragmented market research</h3><p>Buyers often pulled property, demographic, location, and market information from separate sources before creating a usable view.</p></article>
        <article><strong>03</strong><h3>A demonstration needed context</h3><p>A calendar booking had little value unless the buyer arrived with a recognizable use case and knew what the platform would demonstrate.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="saas-truth">The campaign did not sell another database. It showed buyers what becomes possible when the missing property, demographic, and market context appears in one decision.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-targeting">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Target market design</div>
      <h2>The account list followed the decisions the platform could improve.</h2>
      <p class="saas-intro">The strongest prospects were not simply companies connected to real estate. They were organizations and roles that repeatedly evaluated properties, markets, locations, development opportunities, or economic conditions.</p>
      <div class="saas-target-grid">
        <article><span>Primary segment</span><h3>Commercial real estate firms</h3><p>Brokerage, advisory, development, investment, and property teams that needed market intelligence to support opportunities and clients.</p></article>
        <article><span>Secondary segment</span><h3>Location and site selection teams</h3><p>Organizations comparing trade areas, demographic conditions, commercial activity, development potential, and location fit.</p></article>
        <article><span>Public sector fit</span><h3>Economic development teams</h3><p>Municipal and regional organizations using market information to understand communities, attract investment, and support development decisions.</p></article>
        <article><span>Buyer roles</span><h3>Research and revenue leaders</h3><p>Brokers, principals, analysts, development leaders, research directors, location strategists, and other people responsible for market decisions.</p></article>
        <article><span>Useful signals</span><h3>Repeated research workload</h3><p>Multiple markets, property portfolios, active development, client advisory work, site expansion, or a visible need for current location intelligence.</p></article>
        <article><span>Exclusions</span><h3>No credible platform use case</h3><p>Organizations without recurring market decisions, irrelevant roles, unsuitable geography, and contacts with no influence over research or software evaluation.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-message">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The messaging transformation</div>
      <h2>The conversation moved from software capability to decision clarity.</h2>
      <p class="saas-intro">The platform still contained valuable data, reports, maps, and analytical tools. Those capabilities became easier to understand after the buyer recognized the research problem they solved.</p>
      <div class="saas-message-grid">
        <article>
          <span>What created friction</span>
          <h3>The feature led SaaS pitch</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Opening with datasets, dashboards, and platform modules</li>
            <li>Trying to explain every user group in one conversation</li>
            <li>Claiming better data without connecting it to a decision</li>
            <li>Requesting a demo before discovering a real use case</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>What earned attention</span>
          <h3>The missing intelligence question</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>How do you currently research a property or market?</li>
            <li>Which information still has to be assembled manually?</li>
            <li>Where does missing context slow a recommendation?</li>
            <li>Would seeing that decision inside one platform be useful?</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-system">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The SaaS outbound system</div>
      <h2>Five connected steps moved the platform from account list to qualified demo.</h2>
      <div class="saas-system-board">
        <div class="saas-console-head"><span>CALLTEAM SAAS DEMO PATH</span><strong>MARKET TO PRODUCT CONVERSATION</strong></div>
        <div class="saas-system-flow">
          <article><small>01</small><strong>Define the use case</strong><p>Clarify the buyer decisions, platform value, approved claims, ICP, exclusions, demo purpose, and qualification rules.</p></article>
          <article><small>02</small><strong>Build the account market</strong><p>Identify organizations, roles, active markets, research demands, and visible reasons location intelligence could matter.</p></article>
          <article class="active"><small>03</small><strong>Open with the missing insight</strong><p>Connect the call to fragmented research, incomplete context, slow analysis, and the decisions the platform could improve.</p></article>
          <article><small>04</small><strong>Qualify the demonstration</strong><p>Confirm the role, current method, real use case, interest, timing, and what the buyer needed to see.</p></article>
          <article><small>05</small><strong>Book and hand off</strong><p>Schedule the demo with buyer context, use case, questions, current approach, and a clear demonstration objective.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="saas-feedback"><b>↻</b><strong>Live buyer responses improved the segment filters, use case language, qualification questions, demonstration focus, and follow up.</strong></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-qualification">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The qualified demo standard</div>
      <h2>A product demonstration counted only when the buyer had something worth seeing.</h2>
      <div class="saas-gate">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Company fit</h3><p>The organization regularly made property, market, development, or location decisions the platform could support.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Buyer relevance</h3><p>The contact used, influenced, evaluated, or could correctly route market intelligence and software decisions.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Current method</h3><p>The conversation established how research was completed and where information, time, or context was missing.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Recognized use case</h3><p>The buyer could connect the platform to a real market, property, client, development, or location workflow.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Agreed demo purpose</h3><p>The buyer understood why the demonstration was scheduled and what the product team should show.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-timeline">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The 90 day campaign</div>
      <h2>The demo campaign improved as the market revealed which use cases mattered.</h2>
      <div class="saas-timeline-rail">
        <article><span>DAYS 1 TO 15</span><h3>Build the foundation</h3><p>Define the platform value, buyer problems, target segments, roles, exclusions, talk track, qualification, and demo handoff.</p></article>
        <article><span>DAYS 16 TO 30</span><h3>Test the buyer language</h3><p>Launch outreach, compare use cases, inspect objections, correct contact roles, and identify where the product story became unclear.</p></article>
        <article><span>DAYS 31 TO 60</span><h3>Prioritize the strongest fit</h3><p>Focus on the segments, decisions, and buyer roles producing useful conversations while improving follow up and demo context.</p></article>
        <article><span>DAYS 61 TO 90</span><h3>Scale the clearer message</h3><p>Run the refined campaign, protect the qualification standard, coordinate demos, and preserve opportunities requiring more time.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-results">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">The commercial outcome</div>
