    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="saas-lessons">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Why it worked</div>
      <h2>Six decisions made a complex SaaS platform easier to buy.</h2>
      <div class="saas-lesson-grid">
        <article><span>Positioning</span><h3>Decision before dataset</h3><p>The campaign explained the decision the buyer could improve before introducing the platform information behind it.</p></article>
        <article><span>Market</span><h3>Use case before industry label</h3><p>Accounts were selected for recurring property and location decisions, not simply because they belonged to real estate.</p></article>
        <article><span>Conversation</span><h3>Discovery before demonstration</h3><p>Human calling exposed the current research method, missing context, internal route, timing, and reason to continue.</p></article>
        <article><span>Qualification</span><h3>Protect the product team</h3><p>A demo required company fit, buyer relevance, a current method, a recognized use case, and an agreed purpose.</p></article>
        <article><span>Handoff</span><h3>Demonstrate the buyer problem</h3><p>The product team received the situation behind the booking and could show the part of the platform that mattered.</p></article>
        <article><span>Follow up</span><h3>Respect the SaaS sales cycle</h3><p>Opportunities were developed after the first demo instead of treating every initial conversation as an immediate purchase.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="saas-confidentiality"><strong>Client confidentiality:</strong> The company name and identifying details are withheld. The results shown reflect the stated campaign and follow up periods.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

