  <section id="faq" class="saas-faq">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">SaaS appointment setting FAQ</div>
      <h2>Questions about SaaS lead generation, demo booking, and the campaign.</h2>
      <div class="saas-faq-list">
        <details><summary>What is SaaS appointment setting?</summary><p>SaaS appointment setting is the process of identifying suitable companies, reaching the right buyers, explaining the software in relevant business language, qualifying interest, and booking an agreed product demonstration or sales conversation.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How did the campaign book demos for a complex SaaS platform?</summary><p>The campaign translated a complex location intelligence platform into a simple buyer problem. Instead of listing datasets and software features, the conversation focused on fragmented research, missing market context, slow analysis, and the value of seeing property, demographic, and location intelligence together.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Who did the commercial real estate SaaS campaign target?</summary><p>The campaign focused on commercial real estate brokers, developers, site selection professionals, location strategy teams, economic development organizations, and other buyers who regularly needed property, market, location, or demographic intelligence.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What made a SaaS demo qualified?</summary><p>A demo required a suitable organization, a relevant buyer or strong internal route, a real use case for location or market intelligence, enough interest to examine the platform, and clear agreement on the purpose of the demonstration.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How long did the SaaS outbound campaign take?</summary><p>The focused outbound campaign ran for 90 days and booked 11 qualified product demos. Pipeline continued developing after the campaign window, with four opportunities identified and two customers signing within six months.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Which channels can support SaaS demo generation?</summary><p>SaaS demo generation can combine account research, human cold calling, approved email follow up, response handling, qualification, calendar coordination, and structured handoff. The channel mix should match the market, product, sales cycle, and buyer accessibility.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can an outsourced SDR team represent a technical SaaS product?</summary><p>An outsourced SDR team can represent a technical SaaS product when the team understands the buyer problem, use cases, approved claims, qualification rules, common objections, demonstration purpose, and the limits of what should be discussed before a product specialist joins.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What happens after a SaaS demo is booked?</summary><p>The sales team receives the buyer context, use case, relevant pain, current approach, questions, and agreed demonstration purpose. Follow up after the demo then protects longer sales cycles and separates active opportunities from early interest.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
